import{_ as s,C as r,o as l,c as n,j as a,a as i,E as d,a6 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BHBQfcoO.js";const c="/releases/2026.09.0/ha-lcars-theme-lab.webp",h="/releases/2026.09.0/slider-tweens.webm",w=JSON.parse('{"title":"LCARdS 2026.09.0","description":"HA-LCARS Theme Lab, slider marker labels and value tweens, LLM schema bundle.","frontmatter":{"version":"2026.09.0","date":"2026-09-07T00:00:00.000Z","status":"released","summary":"HA-LCARS Theme Lab, slider marker labels and value tweens, LLM schema bundle.","breaking":false},"headers":[],"relativePath":"releases/2026.09.0.md","filePath":"releases/2026.09.0.md"}'),u={name:"releases/2026.09.0.md"};function m(p,e,f,g,b,A){const t=r("ReleaseMeta");return l(),n("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a("h1",{id:"lcards-2026-09-0",tabindex:"-1"},[i("LCARdS 2026.09.0 "),a("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#lcards-2026-09-0","aria-label":'Permalink to "LCARdS 2026.09.0"'},"​")],-1)),d(t),e[1]||(e[1]=o('<p>This release aims to help anyone who&#39;s ever hand-edited HA-LCARS theme YAML: a full visual HA-LCARS theme editor.<br> Plus two solid <code>lcards-slider</code> additions and a handful of important reliability fixes.</p><ul><li><strong>HA-LCARS Theme Lab</strong> - build a complete HA-LCARS-compatible theme visually and export YAML.</li><li><code>lcards-slider</code>: value marker <strong>labels</strong> and a new animation tweens for smooth value transitions</li><li>Sound scheme, MSD, and template-resolution reliability fixes</li><li><strong>LLM Schema + AI-Assistance guide</strong> - help stop your LLM of choice from inventing LCARdS config keys</li></ul><h2 id="new-features" tabindex="-1">New Features <a class="header-anchor" href="#new-features" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;New Features&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="ha-lcars-theme-lab" tabindex="-1">HA-LCARS Theme Lab <a class="header-anchor" href="#ha-lcars-theme-lab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;HA-LCARS Theme Lab&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Building a full HA-LCARS theme can feel daunting with huge YAML files full of unfamiliar variable names. The new <strong>HA-LCARS Theme Lab</strong> found in LCARdS Config panel tries to make this more approachable with a full visual editor.</p><img class="release-shot" alt="HA-LCARS Theme Lab" src="'+c+'"><p>A bit of background on what it&#39;s actually editing:</p><ul><li><strong>Home Assistant doesn&#39;t ask you to pick every colour individually.</strong> Under the hood, HA builds its whole colour system (semantics) from a small handful of <strong>role colours</strong> palettes - <code>primary</code>/<code>neutral</code>/<code>red</code>/<code>orange</code>/<code>green</code> - each with 11 stops. HA takes each role colour palette and computes an entire range of lighter/darker shades from it - button hover states, disabled states, borders, backgrounds, and more. Change the role colour, and every one of those derived shades updates automatically, everywhere.</li><li><strong>HA-LCARS follows the same pattern</strong> for some of its own variables (currently using the <code>primary</code> palette)</li><li>Separately, HA and HA-LCARS also colour things by <strong>entity state</strong> - a light that&#39;s <code>on</code> shows a different colour than <code>off</code>, and different domains (lights, climate, locks, sensors...) have their own conventional colours. This is what&#39;s covered by <strong>Domain &amp; State Colours</strong>.</li></ul><p>Previously, tuning any of this meant knowing exactly which of dozens of <code>--lcars-*</code>/<code>--ha-*</code> variables controlled what, and hand-computing shades yourself. The Theme Lab now handles:</p><ul><li>full light and dark mode selections where supported. You can tune a single theme for both modes (useful if you want to have a daytime and nightime colour profile for your theme)</li><li><strong>Palette Seed</strong> - set each role (<code>primary</code>/<code>neutral</code>/<code>red</code>/<code>orange</code>/<code>green</code>) to use the HA default, one of the bundled LCARdS palettes, a fully customized palette, or by generating a smooth 11-stop shade scale from a single base colour you pick. HA (and HA-LCARS) can then use these palettes for any of their derived colours - exactly like it would from a role colour in a normal HA theme.</li><li>The <strong>Semantic Colours</strong> section includes 83 fields across 8 groups covering HA-LCARS and HA common variables. Thse are searchable and filterable with category chips, and every field has a one-line &quot;applies to&quot; description so you&#39;re not guessing what a variable actually affects.</li><li><strong>Domain &amp; State Colours</strong> matrix - see and override the colours used for entity domains and states (lights, climate, locks, etc.) in one place, instead of hunting through YAML.</li><li><strong>Advanced/Raw Overrides</strong> - a catch-all for any variable outside the curated fields above, for full manual control when you need it.</li><li><strong>Import/export round-trips real theme YAML</strong> - load any HA-LCARS themes present in your HA installation, or if not installed LCARdS ships a current snapshot of HA-LCARS at build time - or paste in your own directly. Fields you haven&#39;t touched show exactly what HA-LCARS itself ships, badged by where the value is actually coming from (HA-LCARS Default / HA Default / Global Default) so you always know if you&#39;re looking at a real override or a fallback.</li><li><strong>Preview &amp; Export</strong> shows your theme applied to real HA components (<code>ha-alert</code>/<code>ha-card</code>/<code>ha-button</code>/<code>ha-switch</code>), real card_mod/UIX-classed demo cards, synthetic-entity domain tiles, and a hand-drawn &quot;LCARS UI Chrome&quot; mockups. There is a full flat swatch sheet and a live YAML export box you can copy straight out.</li></ul><p><strong>This is an evolving feature - please report any issues, discrepancies or ideas!</strong></p><hr><h3 id="slider-value-marker-labels-394-and-transition-animations" tabindex="-1">Slider: Value Marker Labels (<a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/issues/394" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#394</a>) and Transition Animations <a class="header-anchor" href="#slider-value-marker-labels-394-and-transition-animations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Slider: Value Marker Labels ([#394](https://github.com/snootched/lcards/issues/394)) and Transition Animations&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>lcards-slider</code> value markers can now carry an optional <code>label</code> (customizable text/colour/font size/offset), rendered as an SVG caption in both gauge and pills modes. Label text goes through the full template pipeline (JS, Jinja2, token, etc.). Thanks for the suggestions <a href="https://github.com/tswonke" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">@tswonke</a>!</li><li>A new <code>value_tween</code> config block eases the gauge fill, pills opacity sweep, markers and labels between old and new positions whenever (any) bound entity changes on its own - previously all of these snapped instantly. Slider and value markers can be bound to different entities.</li></ul>',14)),e[2]||(e[2]=a("p",null,[a("video",{class:"release-shot",src:h,title:"Slider value tweens and marker labels",autoplay:"",loop:"",muted:"",playsinline:""})],-1)),e[3]||(e[3]=o('<hr><h3 id="llm-schema-bundle-ai-assistance-guide" tabindex="-1">LLM Schema Bundle + AI-Assistance Guide <a class="header-anchor" href="#llm-schema-bundle-ai-assistance-guide" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LLM Schema Bundle + AI-Assistance Guide&quot;">​</a></h3><p>If you&#39;ve ever asked an LLM for LCARdS YAML and gotten back config with invented keys - those models have never seen LCARdS&#39;s real schema. LCARdS now publishes one:</p><ul><li>A JSON Schema bundle (<code>doc/public/lcards-schema.json</code>) covering the standard cards (button, chart, elbow, slider, data-grid, select-menu, alert-overlay, layout-card), generated from the real card schemas and wired into the docs build so it can&#39;t drift.</li><li>A new <a href="/cards/ai-assistance.html">AI-Assistance guide</a> explaining what to attach (the schema bundle + Common Properties + the card&#39;s own doc page) and a prompt template that tells the LLM not to invent properties.</li><li>MSD is excluded for now - its config surface is much deeper and out of scope for now.</li></ul><h2 id="other-fixes-improvements" tabindex="-1">Other Fixes &amp; Improvements <a class="header-anchor" href="#other-fixes-improvements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Other Fixes &amp; Improvements&quot;">​</a></h2><details><summary>Reliability fixes and smaller improvements</summary><ul><li><strong>Sound schemes now survive a full HA restart.</strong> The sound-scheme helper&#39;s options list was synced via a service call that didn&#39;t persist to storage, so an HA restart reloaded stale options and silently fell back to &quot;none&quot; if your selected scheme wasn&#39;t among them. Now written through the proper storage-collection command.</li><li><strong>MSD:</strong> fixed <code>base_svg</code> sources under <code>/local/</code> or <code>http(s)://</code> silently failing to load (&quot;Asset not found&quot;) - <code>builtin:</code> and HA media-library sources were unaffected.</li><li>Hardened <code>ConnectionOverlayService</code>&#39;s post-reconnect config reload against a boot-order race where LCARdS&#39;s own WebSocket commands could still be unregistered for a few seconds after a full HA restart.</li><li><strong>Fixed <code>{states.domain.object_id.state}</code> token resolution</strong> project-wide - the shared token resolver couldn&#39;t reconstruct a dotted entity ID from the flat <code>hass.states</code> dict, so this form silently resolved to an empty string everywhere, not just on the slider.</li><li>Fixed Jinja2 templates missing <code>config</code>/<code>user</code>/<code>variables</code> context, bringing them to parity with JS/token templates.</li><li>Fixed the deprecated anime.js <code>createSpring()</code> (console warning) in favour of <code>spring()</code>; <code>ease: spring</code> now derives its physics from <code>{duration, bounce}</code> so its settling time actually tracks your configured <code>duration</code>.</li><li><code>lcards-form-field</code>: fixed <code>_handleChange</code> to support <code>selectorConfig</code> and normalize empty values.</li><li><code>ThemeManager.getCurrentTheme()</code> (which doesn&#39;t exist) silently broke theme context at 3 call sites - corrected to <code>getActiveTheme()</code>.</li><li><code>AnimationManager._extractValueFromPath()</code> was reading processor buffers from the wrong shape.</li><li>Audited all <code>doc/architecture/**</code> and <code>doc/development/**</code> pages against source and fixed ~30 stale/incorrect API references.</li></ul></details><hr><p>All changes: <a href="https://github.com/snootched/lcards/compare/2026.08.0...2026.09.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">2026.08.0...2026.09.0</a></p>',8))])}const v=s(u,[["render",m]]);export{w as __pageData,v as default};
